      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-221742",
      ":question/problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $10\u2223a$ and $15\u2223a,$ then $150\u2223a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-7}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n10{{field-8}}30\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-9}}30\\,\\text{and}\\,150{{field-10}}30.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-11}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."
    },
