      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-221738",
      ":question/problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\nFor any $a\\in Z,$ if $15\u22236a,$ then $15\u2223a.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nLet's choose $a=$ {{field-6}}.\nThen, we have that \n\n$$\n15{{field-7}}{{30}_{\u23df}}_{6a}\\,\\text{and}\\,15{{field-8}}{{5}_{\u23df}}_{a}.\n$$\n\nSo, $a=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."
